<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "School Project Ideas",
      intro: "Need help with your elephant project? Pick your grade below to find ideas, tips, and " \
             "activities that are just right for you!" %>

<!-- Grade 1 -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>🎨 Grade 1 (Ages 6–7)</h2>
  <p>At this age, projects should be fun, hands-on, and use lots of pictures!</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Draw an Elephant Family</h3>
      <p>
        Draw a picture of an elephant family — a mum, a dad, and a baby calf. Label each one
        and write one fact about each. For example: "The baby weighs as much as a grown-up person!"
      </p>
      <p><strong>You'll need:</strong> Paper, crayons or markers</p>
      <%= copy_button "Draw an Elephant Family" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Fact Poster</h3>
      <p>
        Make a colourful poster with 3 fun facts about elephants. Use big letters and draw or paste
        pictures next to each fact. Present it to your class!
      </p>
      <p><strong>Try these facts:</strong></p>
      <ul style="margin-left: 1.2rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; color: var(--text-body, #3a3a3a);">
        <li>Elephants are the biggest animals on land</li>
        <li>A trunk has 40,000 muscles</li>
        <li>Baby elephants suck their trunks like a thumb</li>
      </ul>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Fact Poster" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>"If I Were an Elephant" Story</h3>
      <p>
        Write 3–5 sentences about what you would do if you were an elephant for a day. Where would
        you go? What would you eat? Who would be in your herd?
      </p>
      <p><strong>Starter:</strong> "If I were an elephant, I would..."</p>
      <%= copy_button '"If I Were an Elephant" Story' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<!-- Grade 2 -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>✏️ Grade 2 (Ages 7–8)</h2>
  <p>Grade 2 projects can include simple writing, comparisons, and beginning research.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant vs. Me</h3>
      <p>
        Make a two-column chart comparing yourself to an elephant. Include: height, weight, what
        you eat, how you sleep, and how you talk to friends. Draw pictures for each row.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Use the</strong> <%= link_to "Bodies & Food", body_and_food_path %> <strong>page for facts!</strong></p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant vs. Me" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Mini-Book</h3>
      <p>
        Fold paper into a small book with 6 pages. Each page covers one topic: what they look
        like, what they eat, where they live, baby elephants, how they talk, and one fun fact.
        Write 2–3 sentences per page with a picture.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Mini-Book" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Three Kinds of Elephants</h3>
      <p>
        Draw all three kinds of elephants (savanna, forest, Asian) side by side. Write one
        sentence about what makes each one special. Use the
        <%= link_to "Family Tree", classification_path %> page to help.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Three Kinds of Elephants" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<!-- Grade 3 -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>📝 Grade 3 (Ages 8–9)</h2>
  <p>Time for paragraphs! Grade 3 projects should have an introduction, details, and a conclusion.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Research Report</h3>
      <p>
        Write a 3-paragraph report about elephants:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-left: 1.2rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; color: var(--text-body, #3a3a3a);">
        <li><strong>Paragraph 1:</strong> What are elephants? (size, where they live)</li>
        <li><strong>Paragraph 2:</strong> Pick one amazing thing about them (trunk, memory, families)</li>
        <li><strong>Paragraph 3:</strong> Why should we protect them?</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use at least 2 pages from this website as sources and say where you found each fact.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Research Report" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Habitat Diorama</h3>
      <p>
        Build a shoebox diorama of an elephant's habitat. Include trees, a water source, and at
        least 2 elephants. Write 5 sentences explaining what you included and why elephants need
        these things. Use the <%= link_to "Life in the Wild", life_in_the_wild_path %> page for ideas.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Habitat Diorama" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Communication Poster</h3>
      <p>
        Create a poster showing all the ways elephants communicate: rumbles, trumpets, body
        language, touch, and ground vibrations. For each one, draw a picture and write what it
        means. Present it to your class.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Communication Poster" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<!-- Grade 4 -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>📊 Grade 4 (Ages 9–10)</h2>
  <p>Grade 4 projects should show deeper research, use data, and include your own thinking.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Species Comparison</h3>
      <p>
        Create a chart or table comparing all 3 elephant species. Include: size, weight, ears,
        tusks, where they live, and how endangered they are. Write a paragraph explaining which
        differences surprised you the most and why.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Use:</strong> <%= link_to "Family Tree", classification_path %> and
        <%= link_to "Bodies & Food", body_and_food_path %></p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Species Comparison" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>"Save the Elephants" Persuasive Letter</h3>
      <p>
        Write a letter to a world leader explaining why elephants need protection. Include at
        least 3 facts about why elephants matter (they spread seeds, dig water holes, are
        intelligent). End with what you think should be done. Use proper letter format.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button '"Save the Elephants" Persuasive Letter' %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Timeline</h3>
      <p>
        Create a timeline of an elephant's life from birth to old age. Include key milestones:
        birth weight, first solid food (6 months), weaning (2–3 years), leaving mum (males at
        12–15 years), first calf (females at 10–12 years), and lifespan (60–70 years).
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Timeline" %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<!-- Grade 5 -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>🔬 Grade 5 (Ages 10–11)</h2>
  <p>Grade 5 projects should include multiple sources, your own analysis, and a clear structure.</p>

  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Research Essay</h3>
      <p>
        Write a 5-paragraph essay on one of these topics:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin-left: 1.2rem; margin-top: 0.3rem; color: var(--text-body, #3a3a3a);">
        <li>How elephant families work and why it matters</li>
        <li>Why elephants are endangered and what can be done</li>
        <li>How elephants compare to humans in intelligence</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Must include:</strong> An introduction, 3 body paragraphs with facts, a conclusion, and a list of sources.</p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Research Essay" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephants in Pop Culture: Fact vs. Fiction</h3>
      <p>
        Pick 3 movies, books, or myths about elephants from the
        <%= link_to "Famous Elephants", root_path %> page. For each one, explain what it gets
        right and what it gets wrong. Write a conclusion about why accurate information matters
        for conservation.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephants in Pop Culture: Fact vs. Fiction" %>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <h3>Elephant Conservation Action Plan</h3>
      <p>
        Research the threats elephants face (poaching, habitat loss, human-wildlife conflict)
        and create an action plan with 3 solutions. For each solution, explain how it works,
        give a real-world example, and say why you think it would help. Present your plan as a
        slideshow or poster.
      </p>
      <%= copy_button "Elephant Conservation Action Plan" %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Teacher tip:</strong> All project ideas can be adapted up or down a grade level. The key
      difference is the depth of research, the amount of writing, and whether students are asked
      to summarise facts or form their own opinions about them.
    </p>
    <%= copy_button "Teacher tip" %>
  </div>
</div>
